# Shipper — local development environment.
#
# Copy to `deploy/.env` and edit. That file is gitignored and must never be committed.
#
#   cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env
#
# Every value below is optional: docker-compose and the Go service both apply the same
# defaults shown here. The file exists so the full set of knobs is discoverable in one
# place, and so overriding one does not require reading the compose file.
#
# The authoritative list for the service is `services/core/internal/config/config.go`.
# If you add a variable there, add it here too — a variable nobody can find is a variable
# nobody sets.
#
# ==============================================================================
# NEVER put a real credential in this file. It is committed.
#
# Production secrets live in the CI secret store and the deployment environment, not in
# the repository (Docs/06 §5.1, §5.2). The credentials below are local-only throwaways
# whose entire security model is that the ports are bound to your own machine.
# ==============================================================================


# --- Service -------------------------------------------------------------------------

# development | staging | production
# Chooses adapter implementations: `development` logs email and SMS to the console instead
# of dispatching them (Docs/06 §4.1, SHIP-32, SHIP-35).
SHIPPER_ENV=development

# Port the Go API listens on.
HTTP_PORT=8080

# Server timeouts. Read and write are deliberately generous enough for proof-of-delivery
# uploads to be *rejected* rather than to hang, but the images themselves go directly to
# object storage via pre-signed URL and never through this service (Docs/06 §5.2).
HTTP_READ_TIMEOUT=15s
HTTP_WRITE_TIMEOUT=30s
HTTP_IDLE_TIMEOUT=120s

# How long in-flight requests get to finish after a shutdown signal before being dropped.
HTTP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=15s


# --- Logging (SHIP-9) ----------------------------------------------------------------

# debug | info | warn | error
LOG_LEVEL=debug

# json | text
# Always `json` outside development — Datadog ingests structured logs (SHIP-174).
# `text` is readable in a terminal and useless to a log aggregator.
LOG_FORMAT=text


# --- PostgreSQL (SHIP-2) -------------------------------------------------------------

# Consumed by docker-compose when creating the container.
POSTGRES_USER=shipper
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=shipper
POSTGRES_DB=shipper
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# Consumed by the Go service and by the migration tooling. Must agree with the four
# variables above. sslmode=disable is correct for a container on loopback and wrong
# everywhere else.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://shipper:shipper@localhost:5432/shipper?sslmode=disable

DATABASE_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=25
DATABASE_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=5
DATABASE_CONN_MAX_LIFETIME=30m


# --- Redis (SHIP-3) ------------------------------------------------------------------

REDIS_PORT=6379
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0


# --- Idempotency (SHIP-15) -----------------------------------------------------------

# How long a completed response stays replayable. This has to outlast the client's retry
# behaviour rather than the request: a phone can be out of coverage for hours and drain
# its queue on the drive home, and every one of those queued operations replays against
# the key it was created with.
IDEMPOTENCY_TTL=24h

# How long a claim survives with no response recorded against it — the answer to "the
# process handling the original request died". Until it lapses, retries of that request
# are refused, so it wants to be comfortably longer than the slowest handler and much
# shorter than IDEMPOTENCY_TTL.
IDEMPOTENCY_IN_FLIGHT_TTL=60s


# --- Password hashing (SHIP-29, SHIP-147a) --------------------------------------------

# RELEASE NOTE — RENAME REQUIRED (SHIP-147a)
#
#   IDENTITY_ARGON2_MEMORY_KIB   is now   PASSWORDS_ARGON2_MEMORY_KIB
#   IDENTITY_ARGON2_ITERATIONS   is now   PASSWORDS_ARGON2_ITERATIONS
#   IDENTITY_ARGON2_PARALLELISM  is now   PASSWORDS_ARGON2_PARALLELISM
#
# Only the names changed. The values, the defaults and the meaning are identical, and no stored
# password is affected — the parameters travel in each hash's PHC string, so every existing hash
# stays verifiable whatever these are set to.
#
# **The old names are not read and are not accepted.** A deployment that leaves them set gets the
# defaults below silently — m=64 MiB, t=3, p=4 — which is the production profile, so nothing
# breaks; but a deployment that had *deliberately raised* the cost falls back to the default
# without any error. Rename them in the secret store in the same change that deploys this
# release, and check the service's startup log line: it prints `argon2=m=…,t=…,p=…` from the
# values actually loaded.
#
# There is one argon2id cost for the whole platform and this is it — a user's password, an
# administrator's password and identity's phone one-time codes are all written at this profile.
# It was named for `identity` until SHIP-147 gave an administrator a password in a domain that
# may not import identity; the hashing lives in `internal/passwords` and the setting now says so.

# argon2id cost. Docs/10 §5 fixes production at m=64 MiB, t=3, p=4 — memory is the parameter
# that does the work, because it is what makes a parallel attack expensive rather than merely
# slow.
#
# These are read only when a password is *written*. Verification takes its parameters from the
# PHC string stored with each hash, so raising them here leaves every existing password
# verifiable and upgrades each one at its owner's next sign-in, with no migration.
PASSWORDS_ARGON2_MEMORY_KIB=65536
PASSWORDS_ARGON2_ITERATIONS=3
PASSWORDS_ARGON2_PARALLELISM=4


# --- Identity: access tokens (SHIP-37) -------------------------------------------------

# How long an issued access token stays valid. Docs/10 §5 fixes it at fifteen minutes.
#
# An access token is not checked against the database, so nothing can shorten one that has
# already been issued — a long value quietly turns "revoke this device" into "revoke this
# device, eventually". Anything over an hour is refused at startup.
IDENTITY_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL=15m

# The HS256 signing keyset, as comma-separated `kid:base64secret` pairs, and the identifier
# of the key that signs new tokens.
#
# Rotation is a configuration change and nothing else: add the incoming key, point
# IDENTITY_ACCESS_TOKEN_ACTIVE_KID at it, and leave the outgoing key in the set until every
# token it signed has expired — fifteen minutes later. Removing it at the moment of the
# switch would sign out every client holding a valid token.
#
#   IDENTITY_ACCESS_TOKEN_KEYS=2026-08:bmV3LWtleS0…,2026-05:b2xkLWtleS0…
#
# Each secret is at least 32 bytes after base64 decoding, because HMAC-SHA256 produces 256
# bits and a key shorter than its own output is the weakest part of the scheme.
#
# THE VALUE BELOW IS A THROWAWAY AND IS PUBLIC — it is in this repository, exactly like the
# database password above. The service refuses to start with it outside development, and
# refuses to start at all in staging or production until this variable is set explicitly
# from the CI secret store (Docs/06 §5.2).
IDENTITY_ACCESS_TOKEN_KEYS=dev:c2hpcHBlci1sb2NhbC1kZXZlbG9wbWVudC1zaWduaW5nLWtleS1ub3QtYS1zZWNyZXQ=
IDENTITY_ACCESS_TOKEN_ACTIVE_KID=dev


# --- Delivery: the driver's job-scoped token (SHIP-107) --------------------------------

# The driver portal is link-authenticated and the driver has no account (Docs/07 §3). What
# they hold is a signed token naming exactly one job, forwarded to them by the provider
# (Docs/01 §4.5).
#
# **This is a second token system and it shares nothing with the one above.** Different key
# material and a different audience — `shipper-driver` against `shipper-mobile` — so neither
# can be exchanged for the other. That is CLAUDE.md's invariant made a property of the
# verifiers rather than a rule somebody remembers. Setting the two keysets to the same secret
# is refused at startup, in every environment including development.

# How long a link works for. Seven days.
#
# Not comparable to the fifteen minutes above, and the difference is the reason it is not:
# there is no refresh. A driver holds no second credential, so this is the whole life of
# their access rather than the short leg of a pair. It has to outlast a delivery — road
# freight on the long-haul corridors is quoted in days — and it does not have to outlast the
# job, because the 72-hour completion window is the customer's and the driver plays no part
# in it. A driver who needs a link after it has lapsed is reissued one (SHIP-109).
#
# Anything over thirty days is refused at startup.
DELIVERY_DRIVER_TOKEN_TTL=168h

# The driver token's own HS256 keyset and active identifier, in the same `kid:base64secret`
# form and rotated the same way.
#
# One difference matters when rotating: an access token lives fifteen minutes, so the
# outgoing key can be dropped within the hour. A driver token lives a week, and dropping its
# key sooner breaks every link already forwarded — with nothing at the other end that can
# refresh. Leave the outgoing key in the set for a full TTL.
#
# THE VALUE BELOW IS A THROWAWAY AND IS PUBLIC, on exactly the same terms as the access token
# keyset above, and it is deliberately a *different* throwaway: sharing one development key
# would leave the two systems separated by the audience alone on every machine anybody works
# on.
DELIVERY_DRIVER_TOKEN_KEYS=driver-dev:c2hpcHBlci1sb2NhbC1kZXZlbG9wbWVudC1kcml2ZXItdG9rZW4ta2V5LW5vdC1hLXNlY3JldA==
DELIVERY_DRIVER_TOKEN_ACTIVE_KID=driver-dev


# --- Email dispatch (SHIP-32, first consumed at SHIP-31) -------------------------------
# SHIPPER_ENV decides which implementation is built, and nothing here overrides that:
# development logs the message to the console and sends nothing, staging and production hand it
# to the provider. A development environment that silently sent real email would announce itself
# only after it had reached somebody's inbox.
#
# **No vendor is named**, and that is the position rather than an omission (Docs/11 §7). The
# adapter speaks a generic contract — a JSON POST under a base URL, with a bearer credential — so
# naming a vendor is setting these three variables and changing no code. Until somebody does,
# the service refuses to start in staging or production, which is the right direction to fail in:
# an account that can never be verified is worse than a deploy that stops.
#
# The base URL is where the vendor's send endpoint lives; the key never appears in a log.
EMAIL_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=
EMAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY=
EMAIL_SENDER=no-reply@shipper.com.au


# --- SMS dispatch (SHIP-35, first consumed at SHIP-34) ---------------------------------
# The same shape as email above, and the default matters more: a text message costs money per
# send and arrives on a real handset, so an environment that dispatched by accident would be a
# bill as well as a nuisance to whoever last used that number for testing.
#
# In development the code is legible in the log on purpose (SHIP-35) — reading it out of the log
# is how a developer verifies a number without a handset.
#
# The sender is an alphanumeric sender ID or an originating number, depending on what the gateway
# and the destination country permit. Australia allows both.
SMS_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=
SMS_PROVIDER_API_KEY=
SMS_SENDER=Shipper

# --- Push notifications (SHIP-139, first consumed at SHIP-140) --------------------------
# Firebase Cloud Messaging, which fronts APNs for iOS as well as delivering to Android.
#
# **Leave PUSH_PROJECT_ID empty and nothing is dispatched**, in any environment: cmd/notifier
# builds the no-op implementation, which records what it would have sent and logs it. That is
# the state of every deployment today, because no Firebase project exists yet.
#
# The fallback leans towards the no-op harder than email's leans towards the console. A machine
# that dispatched by accident would wake a handset belonging to whoever last held that token,
# and unlike an email there is no address to inspect afterwards to work out who.
#
# PUSH_CREDENTIAL is the bearer token presented to FCM. Google issues a short-lived one in
# exchange for a service-account JSON key, and **nothing in this service performs that
# exchange** — it needs golang.org/x/oauth2/google, which is a module this ticket could not
# add. See services/core/internal/platform/push/doc.go. Whatever mints it, it is a credential:
# it belongs in the CI secret store (Docs/06 §5.2) and never in a committed file.
#
# PUSH_BASE_URL overrides Google's endpoint, for a test or a proxy. Empty means
# https://fcm.googleapis.com.
PUSH_PROJECT_ID=
PUSH_BASE_URL=
PUSH_CREDENTIAL=


# --- Geocoding (SHIP-35, first consumed at SHIP-60; configured at SHIP-15g) -------------
# The third adapter of the same shape as email and SMS above: a base URL, a credential, and an
# implementation chosen from SHIPPER_ENV rather than from here.
#
# Empty means no geocoder is configured. Development then builds the console stub, and staging
# and production build nothing at all — addresses are stored unresolved, which every path copes
# with because SHIP-59a requires a failed lookup not to fail the job.
#
# Falling back to the stub outside development was considered and rejected: it returns stable,
# plausible, entirely fictional coordinates, and a staging environment full of those is worse
# than one with none, because it looks like it is working.
#
# Setting the key without the base URL is refused at startup — it is the shape of a
# half-finished configuration whose only symptom would otherwise be silence.
GEOCODING_BASE_URL=
GEOCODING_API_KEY=


# --- Pagination (SHIP-66, configured at SHIP-15g) --------------------------------------
# Docs/10 §4.5 requires these to come from configuration; until SHIP-15g they were constants in
# internal/pagination with a comment saying so. Callers are unaffected — they ask
# pagination.Limit, which reads what cmd/api installs at startup.
#
# The default applies when a request names no ?limit=. The maximum is a ceiling a larger
# ?limit= is narrowed to, never an error, because refusing a page size is a worse client
# experience than quietly giving a smaller page.
#
# Both are bounded 1..500 at load. The maximum is a bound on the work one request can ask of
# the database, so a mistake here is a denial-of-service switch rather than a preference.
PAGINATION_DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE=20
PAGINATION_MAX_PAGE_SIZE=100


# --- Rate limits: the global lever (SHIP-183, SHIP-183a) --------------------------------
# Docs/12 assigns every one of the 86 routes a class and argues each class's figures. These two
# scale every class at once; they are the only rate-limit settings this service reads, and
# Docs/12 §7 explains why there is no per-route override and never should be — 86 routes is 172
# variables nobody maintains correctly, and an override is a number that drifts from the reason
# written beside it with nothing to notice.
#
# BURST_SCALE multiplies every class's capacity: what a cold start or a retry storm can absorb.
# RATE_SCALE multiplies the sustained rate, which divides the interval — at 2.0 a token comes
# back twice as fast. They are separate because they are independent, and the two incidents that
# produce them want different answers: "absorb a bigger burst but hold the same hourly rate" is
# a client-side retry loop, and it is not the same request as "let this NAT through".
#
# Both default to 1.0, which is exactly the figures Docs/12 §3 argues for. Both are bounded
# 0.01..100 at load. Nothing reaches zero: each class keeps a floor of one token and one
# millisecond, so these are a dial rather than an off switch.
#
# Commented out because the default is the document. Set one during an incident, not before.
# RATE_LIMIT_BURST_SCALE=1.0
# RATE_LIMIT_RATE_SCALE=1.0


# --- Object storage: the container (SHIP-15p) -------------------------------------------
# Consumed by docker-compose when creating the MinIO container. The four STORAGE_* credentials
# and the endpoint below must agree with these, exactly as DATABASE_URL agrees with the four
# POSTGRES_* variables.
#
# MinIO speaks the S3 API, so the implementation that runs in staging and production is the one
# exercised locally — against real SigV4 pre-signed URLs rather than a stub.
#
# 9000 is a crowded port on a developer machine (Portainer, SonarQube and php-fpm all want it),
# which is what MINIO_PORT is for. 9001 is the browser console; nothing depends on it, and it is
# what you want the first time an upload lands somewhere unexpected.
#
# THE PASSWORD BELOW IS A THROWAWAY AND IS PUBLIC, on the same terms as the database password.
# It is not `shipper` twice over because MinIO refuses a root password under eight characters.
MINIO_PORT=9000
MINIO_CONSOLE_PORT=9001
MINIO_ROOT_USER=shipper
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=shipperminio


# --- Object storage: the service (SHIP-15p, first consumed at SHIP-114) ------------------
# Proof-of-delivery photographs and provider verification documents. Everything in the bucket
# is private: a client is issued a short-lived pre-signed URL and uploads directly, and the API
# never proxies the bytes (Docs/06 §5.2).

# The S3 API's base URL. **The host in it is signed**, because SigV4 covers the `host` header —
# a URL minted against one address and fetched at another is refused as SignatureDoesNotMatch,
# an error that names neither the address nor the cause. So this is the *published* host port
# and not `minio:9000` inside the compose network: whatever fetches the URL is on the host.
#
# It is always typed, and there is no empty "let the SDK pick the regional endpoint" state: an
# empty variable is an absent one to the loader, so it would fall back to the localhost default
# below and put a deployment on somebody's loopback with no symptom until a retrieval. AWS's own
# is https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com, and a loopback host is refused outside development.
STORAGE_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000

# The bucket, and **this is the one shared service that can be isolated per git worktree**.
#
# A bucket is a cheap namespace, which is exactly what a Kafka topic is not: the stack is shared
# because COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME is pinned, and Kafka has no per-tree equivalent to add, while a
# second bucket costs nothing. Set it per worktree — see CLAUDE.md's worktree table — and
# `make up` creates whatever this names. Unlike TEST_TEMPLATE_DB it is **not** derived from the
# directory, so a worktree that leaves it alone shares this one.
STORAGE_BUCKET=shipper-dev

# The region, which SigV4 signs as part of the credential scope rather than merely routing on.
# The local MinIO container is started with the same value for that reason. Sydney is where the
# deployed bucket will be.
STORAGE_REGION=ap-southeast-2

# Static credentials for the store. Whoever moves the service onto an IAM instance role deletes
# both and lets the SDK find its own.
#
# THE VALUES BELOW ARE THROWAWAYS AND ARE PUBLIC — they are the MINIO_ROOT_* pair above. The
# service refuses to start with them outside development, and refuses to start at all in staging
# or production until both are set explicitly from the CI secret store (Docs/06 §5.2).
STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=shipper
STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=shipperminio

# Address a bucket as `endpoint/bucket/key` rather than `bucket.endpoint/key`.
#
# True locally because the store is MinIO on localhost, where the virtual-host form would need
# `shipper-dev.localhost` to resolve and it does not. A deployment against AWS sets it false.
STORAGE_USE_PATH_STYLE=true

# How long an issued pre-signed **upload** URL works for.
#
# Short, and capped at an hour, because **nothing revokes one once it is signed** — the
# signature is the whole of the authorisation and no server-side check runs when it is redeemed.
# Fifteen minutes is long enough for a phone on a poor connection to finish an upload it has
# already started, which is the only thing it has to outlast.
STORAGE_PRESIGN_TTL=15m

# How long an issued pre-signed **download** URL works for (SHIP-15r).
#
# A separate number, because the two directions have nothing in common but the mechanism and only
# one of them needs to be generous: an upload link has to outlast a transfer that has already
# started, while a download link only has to outlast an image rendering. One value serving both
# made every read link live for as long as an upload needed — and a read link is a live,
# unrevocable link to a photograph of somebody's front door.
#
# Five minutes rather than one, so a tracking view can sit open, a phone can change network
# mid-fetch, and a moderator can open several photographs from one listing without the first
# expiring underneath them. Capped at an hour by the same rule as above, and refused at zero.
STORAGE_DOWNLOAD_TTL=5m

# The largest object an upload URL will be issued for, and the media types one may be issued
# for at all.
#
# Both are policy rather than plumbing, and both live here because Flutter has no
# over-the-air path for Dart code (Docs/06 §5.3): a limit compiled into the client is a limit
# that needs an app release to change, and a proof photograph a driver cannot upload is a
# delivery that cannot be completed.
#
# The client compresses before uploading (Docs/01 §5.2), so ten megabytes is generous rather
# than typical. Bounded 64 KiB..64 MiB at load — below the floor no real photograph fits, and
# above the ceiling the number has stopped describing a photograph.
#
# The type list is a list rather than an `image/*` prefix so that `image/svg+xml`, which is a
# script container browsers execute, cannot arrive by being an image.
STORAGE_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=10485760
STORAGE_ACCEPTED_CONTENT_TYPES=image/jpeg,image/png,image/heic


# --- Provider verification (SHIP-159) ---------------------------------------------------

# How far ahead of its expiry a verification document is worth chasing, per document kind.
#
# **Deliberately unset, and an unset value is a decision rather than an omission.** Docs/04 §3
# states the open question and gives it to somebody else: "which documents are *legally* required
# rather than merely prudent, and **how often each must be renewed**. Owner: legal and insurance
# advisers… This determines expiry tracking (§5) and retention obligations, and remains genuinely
# outside engineering's competence to settle." It is Track-X row X-4 and it is unanswered, so the
# platform ships enforcing no number at all.
#
# Two things are worth keeping apart when reading this:
#
#   * **When a document lapses is not configured here.** It is a fact stated by the provider when
#     they submit the image, held in `provider_verification_documents.expires_at`, and a document
#     that states none never appears on the expiry queue under any setting. Nothing computes an
#     expiry from a submission date.
#   * **This is only how far *ahead* of that date the queue starts asking.** With nothing set, each
#     kind's horizon is "now" — so `GET /v1/admin/moderation/expiring-documents` reports documents
#     that have actually lapsed, and not a day before. Setting a value moves the boundary earlier
#     for that kind, and that number is X-4's to choose.
#
# Comma-separated `kind=duration`, where the kinds are Docs/04 §3's four — licence, registration,
# insurance, abn_evidence — and the duration is a Go duration (`720h` is thirty days). A kind that
# is not one of the four is refused at startup rather than ignored, because configuration that
# silently does nothing looks exactly like a broken feature. A negative value is refused too: it is
# a horizon in the past, and it would hide documents that have already lapsed.
#
# Example, once somebody has decided:
# VERIFICATION_EXPIRY_LEAD_TIMES=insurance=720h,registration=1440h,licence=2160h

# --- Kafka (SHIP-4) ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Host-facing listener. Clients inside the compose network use kafka:9092 instead;
# see the listener comment in docker-compose.yml.
# --- Mobile client (SHIP-167) ---------------------------------------------------------
# The launch-time upgrade gate. Build numbers, not version strings: Docs/07 §8 requires every
# build to carry a monotonically increasing number, which makes this an integer comparison.
#
# Raising a floor is an operational decision with an owner (Docs/07 §6), not a side effect of a
# deploy — which is why it lives here rather than in code. A build below the floor is blocked
# with an update prompt it cannot dismiss, so raising it retires every build beneath it.
MIN_SUPPORTED_IOS_BUILD=1
MIN_SUPPORTED_ANDROID_BUILD=1

# Where a blocked build sends the user. Empty during the pilot: distribution is TestFlight and
# Play internal testing with no public listing (Docs/01 §8), so there is no page to link to yet.
IOS_STORE_URL=
ANDROID_STORE_URL=

# The client policy endpoint, GET /v1/app/policy (SHIP-167a). Two operational numbers the app
# applies on the device, served here because CLAUDE.md puts anything that changes under
# operational pressure server-side and Flutter has no over-the-air path for Dart code.
#
# Both fire on a handset that by assumption has no connection, so the app fetches this while it
# still has signal and keeps the answer. Moving either value reaches a device the next time that
# device is online, not the next time the value is needed.

# Docs/02 §3.1's second rung: how long an update may sit unsynced before the provider is
# prompted to go and find signal. The document is the authority for four hours; this is the dial
# that moves it without a store release.
UNSYNCED_NUDGE_AFTER=4h

# The size a proof photograph is compressed towards on the device before upload (Docs/01 §5.2).
#
# A **budget**, not a bound — STORAGE_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES above is the bound, the size past which
# the platform will not sign an upload at all, and it is ten times this. This is what a driver on
# a metered connection in a yard should be asked to send. Startup refuses a budget above the
# bound: a client aiming higher than the platform accepts compresses to a size guaranteed to be
# refused.
PROOF_COMPRESSION_BUDGET_BYTES=1048576

# --- Tests ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Integration tests clone a template database rather than migrating one per run. `make test`
# builds the template first; pgtest clones it per test binary.
#
# TEST_TEMPLATE_DB is what separates two git worktrees running tests at the same time.
#
# `CREATE DATABASE … TEMPLATE …` resolves the template name at cluster scope, and every
# worktree shares one PostgreSQL on purpose — COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME is pinned so they do. Two
# worktrees with the same template name are therefore two worktrees using the same database:
# one `make test` drops and rebuilds it while the other is midway through cloning it.
#
# **Leave it unset.** The Makefile derives it from the directory name, so a worktree is
# isolated without anybody remembering — which matters because `git worktree add` does not
# create this file, and the cost of forgetting lands on the *other* worktree.
# TEST_TEMPLATE_DB=shipper_test_template_something
#
# TEST_DATABASE_URL only helps when a worktree points at an entirely separate PostgreSQL. On
# a shared cluster the template is still resolved by name, so this changes nothing on its own
# (Docs/10 §7.1). It defaults to DATABASE_URL.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://shipper:shipper@localhost:5432/shipper?sslmode=disable
# TEST_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/1

KAFKA_PORT=29092
KAFKA_BROKERS=localhost:29092

# How many copies of each partition `make topics` creates the topic set with.
#
# One is correct for this single-broker compose stack and wrong everywhere else — a deployed
# cluster wants 3, and a topic created with one replica loses its events when that broker
# does. It is the only number in the topic set that differs between environments; the
# partition count and the retention belong to the event catalogue and are the same on every
# broker.
#
# `go run ./cmd/topics -replication N` overrides this for a one-off run against a cluster.
# Bounded 1..10 at load, which is a typo guard rather than a limit: a factor larger than the
# cluster is refused by the broker itself, and nothing here can know how many brokers it has.
KAFKA_REPLICATION_FACTOR=1

# Fixed so the broker's storage directory survives a container recreation. Changing it
# after first start orphans the existing log directory — run `make reset` if you change it.
KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID=5L6g3nShT-eMCtK--X86sw
